<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huastec-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huastec people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huastec, or Teenek, are an Indigenous people of north-eastern Mexico who speak a Mayan language that separated from the other Mayan languages long ago.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huasteca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huasteca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huasteca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huasteca is a region on the Gulf coast of Mexico, covering parts of Veracruz, San Luis Potosí, Tamaulipas, Hidalgo, Querétaro and Puebla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huave"><span class="enc-name">Huave</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huave, who call themselves Ikoots, are an Indigenous people of the Pacific coast of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec in Oaxaca, Mexico, living around coastal lagoons…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huayna Capac</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Huayna Capac was the Sapa Inca who ruled the Inca Empire at its greatest extent, from about 1493.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubal</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubal was a god worshipped in pre-Islamic Mecca, whose statue of red stone with a golden hand stood inside the Kaaba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubei</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubei is a province of central China on the middle Yangtze, with its capital at Wuhan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-ingraham"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-ingraham.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Ingraham</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Ingraham is a Bahamian politician who served as prime minister of the Bahamas from 1992 to 2002 and again from 2007 to 2012.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-lyautey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Lyautey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Lyautey was a French general and the first resident-general of the French protectorate in Morocco, from 1912 to 1925.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-maga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Maga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Maga was a schoolteacher from northern Dahomey who became one of the colony's three leading politicians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-murray"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-murray.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Murray</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Murray was an Australian lawyer who governed Papua as lieutenant-governor from 1908 until his death in 1940.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huchuquan"><span class="enc-name">Huchuquan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Huchuquan was the last effectively independent chanyu of the Southern Xiongnu, ruling from 195 to 216.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudhayl-ibn-khalaf-ibn-razin"><span class="enc-name">Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin was a Berber chief of the Banu Razin who made Albarracín independent around 1012, as the Caliphate of Córdoba broke apart.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-bay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson Bay</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hudson Bay is a large inland sea in north-eastern Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson River flows about 500 kilometres south from the Adirondack Mountains of New York to the Atlantic at New York City.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-valley"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-valley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson Valley</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson Valley is the valley of the Hudson River in New York, running north from New York City towards Albany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudsons-bay-company.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson's Bay Company</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson's Bay Company was chartered by Charles II in 1670 with a monopoly of trade in all lands draining into Hudson Bay, a territory called Rupert's Land.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudud-al-alam"><span class="enc-name">Hudud al-Alam</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudud al-Alam, 'The Limits of the World', is an anonymous Persian geography completed in 982 and dedicated to the Farighunid amir Abu'l-Harith Muhammad of Guzgan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hue"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hue.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huế</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Huế, formerly Phu Xuan, is a city on the Perfume River in central Vietnam.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huelva"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huelva.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huelva</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Huelva is a port city in Andalusia, south-western Spain, where the Odiel and Tinto rivers meet, and capital of the province of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huerta"><span class="enc-name">Huerta</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A huerta is an irrigated agricultural area in Spain, especially around Valencia, Murcia and Granada, using irrigation channels developed in the Islamic period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huerva"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huerva.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huerva</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huerva is a river of Aragon in north-eastern Spain, a right-bank tributary of the Ebro about 130 km long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huesca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huesca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huesca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Huesca is a city in Aragon in north-eastern Spain, at the foot of the Pyrenees.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huete"><span class="enc-name">Huete</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Huete is a town in the province of Cuenca, Castile-La Mancha, Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huexotzinca"><span class="enc-name">Huexotzinca</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huexotzinca were the people of Huexotzinco, an independent city-state in central Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huey-tlatoani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huey-tlatoani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huey Tlatoani</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Huey Tlatoani, meaning Great Speaker, was the title of the supreme ruler of Tenochtitlan and the Aztec Triple Alliance.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hug-roger-iii-of-pallars-sobira"><span class="enc-name">Hug Roger III of Pallars Sobirà</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hug Roger III was the last count of Pallars Sobirà in the Catalan Pyrenees.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-capet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh Capet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh Capet was Duke of the Franks and, from 987, King of the Franks, the founder of the Capetian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh d'Avranches, Earl of Chester</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh d'Avranches was a Norman nobleman, a companion of William the Conqueror, who became Earl of Chester in 1071.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-earl-of-ulster"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-de-lacy-earl-of-ulster.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh de Lacy, Earl of Ulster</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh de Lacy, a younger son of the Lord of Meath, was made Earl of Ulster by King John in 1205 after he had driven out John de Courcy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh de Lacy was an Anglo-Norman baron who in 1172 received from Henry II the former Kingdom of Mide as the Lordship of Meath, to balance Strongbow's power in Leinster.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-ii-of-jaffa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-ii-of-jaffa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh II of Jaffa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh II of Le Puiset (c. 1106–1134) was Count of Jaffa in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and a cousin of Queen Melisende.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-iii-duke-of-burgundy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-iii-duke-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh III was duke of Burgundy from 1162 to 1192.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh IV was duke of Burgundy from 1218 to 1272.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-low"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-low.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh Low</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh Low was a British colonial administrator and naturalist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-maguire-lord-of-fermanagh"><span class="enc-name">Hugh Maguire, Lord of Fermanagh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh Maguire was Lord of Fermanagh from 1589 and one of the Irish leaders of the Nine Years' War against English rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-montgomery-1st-viscount-montgomery"><span class="enc-name">Hugh Montgomery, 1st Viscount Montgomery</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh Montgomery, 1st Viscount Montgomery, was a Scottish laird from Ayrshire who in 1605, with James Hamilton, acquired most of the lands of Con O'Neill of Clandeboye…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone, was the most powerful Gaelic lord of Ulster at the end of the 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-of-arles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Arles</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Arles, also called Hugh of Provence, was count of Arles and Vienne and effective ruler of Provence before becoming King of Italy in 926.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-bruyeres"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Bruyeres</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Bruyeres was a crusader knight from Champagne who received the Barony of Karytaina, one of the largest fiefs of the Principality of Achaea, around 1209.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-ibelin"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Ibelin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Ibelin was the eldest son of Barisan of Ibelin and lord of Ibelin and Ramla in the Kingdom of Jerusalem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-montgomery-2nd-earl-of-shrewsbury"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-of-montgomery-2nd-earl-of-shrewsbury.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Montgomery, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Montgomery was a Norman nobleman, son of Roger of Montgomery, who became Earl of Shrewsbury in 1094.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-saint-omer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-of-saint-omer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Saint-Omer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Saint-Omer, also called Hugh of Fauquembergues, was a knight from Artois who came east with the First Crusade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-tuscany"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hugh-of-tuscany.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Tuscany</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh, also called Hugh the Great, was Margrave of Tuscany from the 960s and for a time Duke of Spoleto and Camerino, a loyal supporter of the Ottonian emperors in Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-vermandois"><span class="enc-name">Hugh of Vermandois</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh of Vermandois was a younger son of King Henry I of France who became Count of Vermandois by marriage to its heiress Adelaide.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-roe-macmahon"><span class="enc-name">Hugh Roe MacMahon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh Roe MacMahon was lord of Monaghan, the MacMahon lordship of Oriel in Ulster.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great"><span class="enc-name">Hugh the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hugh the Great was a Robertian count of Paris and Duke of the Franks, the most powerful noble of West Francia in the 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hughes-peynado-plan"><span class="enc-name">Hughes–Peynado plan</span><span class="enc-type">Event</spa